From: System Electronics
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 12:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/bridge: cdns-dsi: fix phy de-init and flag it so

Backport of the upstream patch by Aradhya Bhatia (TI), part of the series
"drm/bridge: cdns-dsi: Fix the color-shift issue", v11 02/14, March 2025.
Fixes: fced5a364dee ("drm/bridge: cdns: Convert to phy framework").
Reviewed-by and Tested-by: Dmitry Baryshkov, Tomi Valkeinen.

Upstream text:

  The driver code doesn't have a Phy de-initialization path as yet, and so
  it does not clear the phy_initialized flag while suspending. This is a
  problem because after resume the driver looks at this flag to determine
  if a Phy re-initialization is required or not. It is in fact required
  because the hardware is resuming from a suspend, but the driver does not
  carry out any re-initialization causing the D-Phy to not work at all.

Three adaptations to our 5.15 tree, all verified before writing this:

  1. The file is drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/cdns-dsi.c, not
     drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/cadence/cdns-dsi-core.c. The split into a
     subdirectory is later than our kernel.
  2. The callback is cdns_dsi_bridge_post_disable(), without the atomic_
     prefix.
  3. Our driver does use the phy framework: dsi->dphy comes from
     devm_phy_get(dev, "dphy") and cdns_dsi_hs_init() calls phy_init(),
     phy_set_mode(), phy_configure() and phy_power_on(). phy_power_off()
     and phy_exit() appeared nowhere in the file, so the bug described
     upstream is literally ours and the patch applies as written.

Our cdns_dsi_suspend() cleared both flags, where upstream cleared one.
Both are removed here. phy_init() and phy_power_on() are reference
counted: clearing the flags without the matching phy_exit() and
phy_power_off() leaves those counts permanently high, so ops->exit would
never run again. Flags and PHY calls now live together in post_disable().

Known hole, deliberately left: a system suspend with the display still on,
one that does not go through post_disable(), would resume with stale
flags. That does not happen on this board.

One local addition on top of the upstream patch: pm_runtime_put() becomes
pm_runtime_put_sync_suspend(). The upstream patch clears the flags and
de-initializes the PHY, but the clocks and the reset line are touched only
by cdns_dsi_suspend(). Without a synchronous suspend the caller cannot
know the block was really cycled, and the userspace workaround this
replaces had to sleep and poll runtime_status to find out. put_sync alone
would not do: it runs the idle callback and does not guarantee reaching
suspend. Same reasoning and same call as upstream commits
"drm/bridge: anx7625: Ensure bridge is suspended in disable()" and
"drm/bridge: parade-ps8640: Ensure bridge is suspended in .post_disable()".

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/cdns-dsi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/cdns-dsi.c
@@ -964,7 +964,26 @@
 	struct cdns_dsi_input *input = bridge_to_cdns_dsi_input(bridge);
 	struct cdns_dsi *dsi = input_to_dsi(input);
 
-	pm_runtime_put(dsi->base.dev);
+	/*
+	 * The driver had no PHY de-initialization path at all: cdns_dsi_hs_init()
+	 * called phy_init() and phy_power_on() and nothing ever undid them, so
+	 * phy_initialized stayed true and the D-PHY was never rebuilt. Undo it
+	 * here, where the display really goes down, and clear both flags so the
+	 * next pre_enable reconstructs link and PHY from scratch.
+	 */
+	dsi->phy_initialized = false;
+	dsi->link_initialized = false;
+	phy_power_off(dsi->dphy);
+	phy_exit(dsi->dphy);
+
+	/*
+	 * Synchronous, and specifically _suspend: plain pm_runtime_put_sync()
+	 * only runs the idle callback and does not guarantee we reach
+	 * cdns_dsi_suspend(), which is what gates the clocks and asserts the
+	 * reset line. Without that guarantee a caller has to sleep and poll
+	 * runtime_status to know the block was really cycled.
+	 */
+	pm_runtime_put_sync_suspend(dsi->base.dev);
 }
 
 static const struct drm_bridge_funcs cdns_dsi_bridge_funcs = {
@@ -1217,8 +1236,15 @@
 	clk_disable_unprepare(dsi->dsi_sys_clk);
 	clk_disable_unprepare(dsi->dsi_p_clk);
 	reset_control_assert(dsi->dsi_p_rst);
-	dsi->link_initialized = false;
-	dsi->phy_initialized = false;
+	/*
+	 * link_initialized and phy_initialized are deliberately not cleared
+	 * here. phy_init() and phy_power_on() are reference counted, and
+	 * clearing the flags without the matching phy_exit()/phy_power_off()
+	 * leaves those counts permanently high, so ops->exit would never run
+	 * again. Flags and PHY calls live together in
+	 * cdns_dsi_bridge_post_disable(), which is also the only path that
+	 * reaches this function.
+	 */
 	return 0;
 }
 
